<DocsLayout title="Introduction" activeSlug="introduction" description="What PAS-Agent is: a local-first CLI that keeps a portable record of coding work so you can hand it from one AI coding agent to another.">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>
    PAS-Agent is an open-source, local-first tool for preserving and transferring
    coding work between AI coding agents. <strong>PAS</strong> stands for
    <strong>Portable Agent Sessions</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you switch from one coding agent to another, say from Claude Code to
    Codex, the context you built up doesn't come with you. The task, the decisions,
    the files you changed, and what comes next all have to be explained again.
  </p>
  <p>
    PAS-Agent records that context in a provider-neutral session file and exports it
    into the instruction file the next agent already reads at startup
    (<code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, or <code>GEMINI.md</code>).
  </p>

  <h2>The Core Idea</h2>
  <pre><code>Claude Code
    ↓
pas-agent update / checkpoint
    ↓
.pas-agent/session.json   (portable session state)
    ↓
pas-agent export --to codex
    ↓
Codex reads AGENTS.md and continues the same work</code></pre>

  <h2>What PAS-Agent Is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Local-first</strong>: all data stays in your project directory</li>
    <li><strong>Provider-neutral</strong>: exports for Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Kiro CLI, and Antigravity</li>
    <li><strong>CLI-first</strong>: a single Rust binary, no daemon</li>
    <li><strong>Non-destructive</strong>: exports go into a marked block, so your own instructions are never overwritten</li>
    <li><strong>Open source</strong>: MIT licensed</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What PAS-Agent Is Not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not a replacement coding agent</li>
    <li>Not a cloud service</li>
    <li>Not a parser for agents' proprietary session formats</li>
    <li>Not an orchestration framework</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Current Status</h2>
  <p>
    PAS-Agent is in <strong>Phase 1 (Foundation)</strong>. The session model,
    CLI, checkpoints, and context export are implemented, and you update the session
    by hand. Phase 2 will add shell hooks that update and export automatically when you
    switch agents.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
